WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.090
Speaker 1: Let's go to David from Colorado.

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.670
David, you're on the air next.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:05.670
Speaker 6: Hey, David, can.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:06.670
Speaker 1: You hear me?

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:07.670
I can hear you fine.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:08.730
Speaker 6: Great.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:15.430
I wanted to talk quickly about democratic
ideals, given that so many Supreme Court justices

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:21.039
have been appointed by presidents that have
not won the popular vote.

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:22.039
Correct.

00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:25.410
Do you support the Supreme Court still as
an institution?

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:28.570
Do you believe it has a rightful place in
our democracy?

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:33.929
Speaker 1: I believe it has a rightful place
in our democracy, but we need to be confident

00:00:33.929 --> 00:00:34.929
in it.

00:00:34.929 --> 00:00:39.199
And we did a story on the Wednesday show about
how confidence in the Supreme Court has collapsed,

00:00:39.199 --> 00:00:41.079
and it's even worse than you're pointing out.

00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:47.620
So David correctly points out right now five
of the nine justices were chosen, were nominated

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:51.000
to the court by presidents who lost the popular
vote.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:59.589
So it was Roberts and Alito by Bush and then
Gorsuch, Coney Barrett and Kavanaugh by Trump.

00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:04.850
That's five of the nine justices were chosen
by presidents where more people voted for

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:05.850
their opponents.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.030
But it's even worse than that, David.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:11.590
In addition to that, one of them quite literally
should have been Barack Obama.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:15.810
Selection Gorsuch should have actually been
Obama's nomination of Merrick Garland.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:19.400
Mitch McConnell got in the way of that, but
then it's even worse than that.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:25.270
Not to really depress you today, David, but
also the completely anti-democratic Senate

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:31.450
where Wyoming gets two senators and California
gets two senators, even though California

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.320
has 30 something million people and Wyoming
has not even a million people.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:38.820
They are the ones who voted to confirm these
people.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.700
So it's a completely anti-democratic process.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.979
And I understand why there is very little
confidence in the court right now, but.

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:52.490
If we could make its makeup more legitimate
as an institution, I do still think that having

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:54.310
a Supreme Court is an important thing.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:55.310
Speaker 6: Right?

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:56.310
Right.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:03.190
And I'm totally with you in terms of the antidemocratic
institutions within our system between the

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:04.940
Electoral College and Senate.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:06.070
I'm actually from Wyoming.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:07.070
Okay.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:08.070
Move things to.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:12.280
Speaker 1: No offense to Wyoming, but listen,
why do you guys have two senators, you know?

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:13.349
Speaker 6: Yeah.

00:02:13.349 --> 00:02:15.370
No, it's completely ridiculous.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:17.349
Yeah, in my opinion.

00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:22.010
But do you have any specific reforms in mind?

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:28.790
Something that was talked about during the
2020 campaign by people to judge was a possible

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:36.980
make up in which maybe five justices are appointed
by the conservative nominee, or rather conservative

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.720
presidential candidates.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:44.560
Half or five are nominated by the liberal
presidential candidates, and then the remaining

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:50.280
five are nominated by the ten seated justices.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.189
Do you have anything like that in mind or
any other systems?

00:02:53.189 --> 00:02:56.099
Speaker 1: I am open to a lot of reforms.

00:02:56.099 --> 00:02:59.669
One idea that is, I think similar to what
you're suggesting, but not exactly it, is

00:02:59.669 --> 00:03:04.370
that you always have a54 court and it's just
whoever's in power in the White House gets

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:06.769
the five rather than the four.

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:09.889
That's one idea that I've seen floated term
limits is another.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:16.629
Obviously, I would love it if the presidents
choosing the justices won the popular vote

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:20.689
and we could actually say they actually were
reflective of the will of the people.

00:03:20.689 --> 00:03:23.349
But that requires getting rid of the Electoral
College.

00:03:23.349 --> 00:03:29.219
The other aspect to this is there can't be
if you're president, it I think we have to

00:03:29.219 --> 00:03:30.219
just set a line.

00:03:30.219 --> 00:03:35.769
And even if you have a week left and you nominate
someone, I think that they need to get a confirmation

00:03:35.769 --> 00:03:36.769
hearing.

00:03:36.769 --> 00:03:40.209
I don't think you can even there it shouldn't
be an option for Mitch McConnell and Republicans

00:03:40.209 --> 00:03:42.049
to do what they did.

00:03:42.049 --> 00:03:44.519
Speaker 6: No, I totally agree.

00:03:44.519 --> 00:03:45.519
Yeah.

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:46.519
Final question on Roe.

00:03:46.519 --> 00:03:50.519
Do you think this could be a straw that broke
the camel's back in terms of Democrats winning

00:03:50.519 --> 00:03:51.519
back a supermajority?

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:53.889
Speaker 1: We've talked about that.

00:03:53.889 --> 00:03:54.889
I don't know yet.

00:03:54.889 --> 00:04:00.989
I think that this will probably inspire some
turnout for Democrats in November.

00:04:00.989 --> 00:04:02.530
But I don't know about a supermajority.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:03.629
That seems like a bit of a stretch.

00:04:03.629 --> 00:04:05.290
Speaker 6: I totally get that.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:09.499
Speaker 1: I think not losing would be I think
not losing by as much would be like probably

00:04:09.499 --> 00:04:10.879
the best they can do.

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:13.129
Speaker 6: Yeah.

00:04:13.129 --> 00:04:15.189
We'll see what happens in November, I suppose.

00:04:15.189 --> 00:04:16.209
Absolutely gets it.

00:04:16.209 --> 00:05:20.830
Speaker 1: David from Colorado, thank you
so much 

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:29.460
for 
the call.

